they are to be known by you, O king, as the fourteen worlds. / In the liṅga is the god Maheśvara; the round pillar is the Grandfather [Brahmā];
and the pillar which is square is the god Janārdana. / Likewise, the three auspicious pedestals are to be understood as the nature of the three qualities [guṇas];
they are said to be the receptacle of the qualities, the triple world with the moving and unmoving. / Beneath the worlds, and above the liṅga, O king,
the guardians of the world should be made, with tridents in their hands, in the four directions: / Virūḍha, Dhṛtarāṣṭra, and Virūpākṣa, O Yādava,
and Kubera of great splendour, auspicious, wearing the attire of Sūrya; / all should be made clad in armour and adorned with auspicious ornaments.
Know Virūḍhaka [to be] the god Śakra, the lord of the Gaṇas; / know Dhṛtarāṣṭra [to be] Yama, the ruler of the world;
know Virūpākṣa [to be] Varuṇa, the lord of aquatic creatures; / know the king of kings Kubera [to be] the lord who bestows wealth.

3.18.m:
it should be furnished with diverse figures, neither round nor trident-like. / But eight spires should be made there provided with niches;
adorned with niches accompanied by latticed windows, O king. / Everywhere the spires should be made with auspicious āmalaka finials,
provided with wheels, pennants, and banners, O ruler of men. / Raised high, this temple shines as though clinging to the sky.
Learn from me the placement of deities in this temple in proper sequence: / Vāsudeva should be made here facing the eastern direction, O king,
or facing the western direction, but not facing any other quarter. / And the direction of Vāsudeva here should be considered the east, O king;
and the determination of the directions should be made in accordance with it. / The lord god Saṃkarṣaṇa should be made facing the southern direction;
and next, Pradyumna should be made facing the western direction; / Aniruddha, O king, is prescribed facing the northern direction.

